  "worked_solution": "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-4$ and $x=8.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/56ec4a3e3bd4b8b652832d4b32ba730717a49ab2438ef2c978ea99b291a7f462.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $f(x)=x+6.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-4}^{8}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{8}[\\,(x+6)-(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{8}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+x+8)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
